  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A flame burns behind a waterfall in New York. Shale Creek Preserve, in Chestnut Ridge Park, south of Buffalo. A thirty-foot drop, and fire underneath.",
        "visual_query": "western new york waterfall gorge",
        "visual_fallback": "small forest waterfall"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Gas seeps from a crack in the rock at the base. Hikers light it; spray and wind put it out; someone lights it again.",
        "visual_query": "rock crevice gas flame shale",
        "visual_fallback": "small flame rock"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2013 geologists sampled it. Thirty-five percent ethane and propane, far richer than an ordinary seep. The source: Rhinestreet Shale, four hundred metres down.",
        "visual_query": "appalachian shale rock layers",
        "visual_fallback": "layered shale cliff"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Here's the problem. Shale normally needs about a hundred degrees Celsius to break into gas. This shale is cooler, younger, shallower.",
        "visual_query": "geologist sampling rock outcrop",
        "visual_fallback": "rock outcrop closeup"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The leading explanation is a catalyst in the rock, splitting hydrocarbons at low temperature. One kilogram of methane a day feeds one small flame.",
        "visual_query": "shale gas seep flame rock",
        "visual_fallback": "gas flame stone"
      }
    ],
